jerome powell was recently on a panel  and he was asked about inflation and the  health of the u.s economy and in it he  said we now understand more how much we  don't understand inflation now that  exact quote probably has your brain in a  blunder asking how the hell does the  central bank not understand that if they  print a bunch of money and they go ahead  and manipulate interest rates down that  inflation would take off well here's  exactly what jerome had to say  i think we now understand better how  little we understand  about it that's not very reassuring no  you know honestly this was this was  unpredicted at the time of our june  meeting one year ago  the of the 35  people who filed  with this survey of professional  forecasters 34 of them had inflation  below 4 for last year of course it was  way above four percent so really  everyone had the same model which was  the phillips curve model and it just was  not capable of producing high inflation  but what it was missing was something  that's completely missing in the data  for 40 years which is basically a  collapse of the supply side you know the  the us economy is famously adaptable you  know it does it has the minimum of  structural rigidities all that kind of  thing and yet here they are so you so  what you had was very strong demand but  hitting effectively a vertical supply  curve so ordinarily when people want to  buy cars which they really did because  they didn't want to ride on public  transportation rates were low they had  all these savings  the car companies would make more cars  and they might raise prices too in this  case  they couldn't make as many cars so so  what you got was straight up the  vertical supply curve a big price  increase but i also think in principle  at least that process could work in  reverse so that as demand comes down you  know inflation could actually come down  more quickly than would be then would  you know there are other relationships  that people think about when they think  about how do you get inflation down  which are more typical of a simply  overheated economy and an entrenched  inflation the sacrifice ratio and those  kinds of things but i think this could  be i don't know that it will be but it  could be different because it's just  that process working in reverse now it's  important to call out that of course  this is hard the u.s economy and the  global economy they're complex machines  to anticipate that any one person any  one central bank or any one group of  individuals could actually understand  all of the different levers the inputs  the outputs and what the impact of every  single decision could be is nearly  impossible i don't envy these people i  don't think that i could do any better  job but that ultimately ends up being  the argument is that should humans  actually be driving monetary policy  should humans be driving fiscal policy  especially when it is in response and it  is reactive to market conditions we got  intense loose monetary policy and also  immense fiscal policy stimulus when  there was a market downturn due to the  kovid 19 crisis  now all of a sudden back in november of  2021 when asset prices were running to  the sky  we got a federal reserve that stepped in  and decided that they wanted to crash  asset prices they wanted to destroy  demand and they simply wanted to try to  bring inflation down  many of these policy makers many of  these decision makers they simply are  using data that is backwards looking  all of that inflation data is telling  you what happened over the last 12  months but it isn't necessarily  effective or helpful to tell you what's  going to happen in the next 12 months to  come and so what we have are humans  using backwards looking data to make  decisions today that affect our future  this is a very hard problem and it's one  that frankly i don't think anyone can do  perfectly and so ultimately the question  becomes should we have an algorithm  something that doesn't change simply do  the job  if we can automate monetary policy  there's a lot of people especially in  the bitcoin community that believes that  it would be easier for individuals and  businesses to plan their financial  futures it's interesting to see jerome  powell the chairman of the federal  reserve start talking so openly and  honestly about some of the mistakes of  monetary and fiscal policy in the united  states  i tend to think that what we're seeing  now is its true thoughts and it's  encouraging that at least we're getting  a more raw look into the thought process  and the emotions of some of these  decision makers the chairman of federal  reserve and the treasury secretary they  aren't elected positions in fact they're  simply appointed and they have bosses  which are the politicians that appointed  them and so naturally when you have a  position where you don't answer to the  american people there's a lot of people  who ask the question  should they be elected officials i don't  necessarily think that's where we should  go  but i do think that journalists and the  american public should be asking much  tougher questions of these individuals  where did we go wrong  why did we go wrong and how can we  ensure that we have the ability to make  sure that we get back on track and that  we should be able to trust  that the united states can get inflation  down and that we can stop any sort of  spiral into a recession i don't have  those answers i have way more questions  than answers but i do know that now  we're starting to get a tone  from our policymakers from those people  in positions of power and influence it's  very different than what we heard in  2020 and most of 2021
 

